<p>New Market's housing stock reflects its character. There are homes that have been in families for generations — older construction, often with additions built over the years, rooflines that weren't designed for modern drainage expectations. There are also newer homes scattered throughout, people who wanted land and quiet without the Huntsville price tag.</p>

<p><strong>What we see out here:</strong></p>

<p><strong>The farmhouse situation:</strong> Older homes often have fascia boards that have seen better days. Original gutters — if they exist — are sectional, leaking at every joint, and held up by rusting hangers. Sometimes "gutters" means a board nailed under the roofline with varying degrees of success.</p>

<p><strong>The DIY history:</strong> Rural homeowners are resourceful. We've seen creative gutter repairs involving everything from tar to screen wire to sections of downspout wired together. We don't judge — we just replace it with something that works.</p>

<p><strong>The tree factor:</strong> North Madison County has serious tree coverage. Hardwoods, pines, whatever's been growing for the past 50 years. That means debris — lots of it — falling into gutters from spring through winter.</p>
